          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Calcium hydroxide + Carbon dioxide Calcium carbonate + Water (Lime water) (Insoluble substance)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.122,
            0.744,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The turning of lime water milky is also used as a test for carbon dioxide. Let us explore this with another activity using some substances from our kitchen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.189,
            0.442,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.4: Let us experiment"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.221,
            0.729,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a teaspoonful of vinegar or lemon juice in a test tube."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.244,
            0.743,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Add a pinch of baking soda (sodium hydrogen carbonate) to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.285,
            0.379,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.306,
            0.743,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You would hear a fizzing bubbling sound and see the gas bubbles forming."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.347,
            0.743,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pass this gas through freshly prepared lime water kept in another test tube, as shown in Fig. 5.3a."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.389,
            0.379,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.41,
            0.744,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The lime water turns milky. What do you "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "infer",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " about the gas formed by mixing vinegar and baking soda?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.45,
            0.627,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This indicates that the gas formed is carbon dioxide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.472,
            0.743,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This activity can also be performed using two small used bottles instead of test tubes and a flexible straw, as shown in Fig. 5.3b."
            }
          ]
        },
